      "description": "Side-by-side comparison showing the dramatic 12:1 self-loop asymmetry between perfect and imperfect consonances.",
      "html": "<!DOCTYPE html><html lang='en'><head><meta charset='UTF-8'><meta name='viewport' content='width=device-width,initial-scale=1'><title>Bottleneck Visualizer</title><style>*{margin:0;padding:0;box-sizing:border-box}body{font-family:'Segoe UI',system-ui,sans-serif;background:#0a0a1a;color:#e0e0e0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:center;padding:30px;min-height:100vh}h1{font-size:1.5rem;margin-bottom:5px;color:#7eb8da}h2{font-size:.9rem;font-weight:normal;color:#888;margin-bottom:30px}.row{display:flex;gap:40px;flex-wrap:wrap;justify-content:center}.card{background:#1a1a2e;border-radius:16px;padding:25px;width:340px;text-align:center}.card h3{font-size:1.2rem;margin-bottom:15px}.card.perfect{border:2px solid #b8860b}.card.imperfect{border:2px solid #008b8b}.grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(4,1fr);gap:8px;margin:20px auto;max-width:240px}.cell{width:50px;height:50px;border-radius:8px;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-size:.75rem;font-weight:bold;transition:all .3s}.cell.active{transform:scale(1.05)}.cell.forbidden{background:#2a1a1a;color:#ff4444;border:1px solid #ff4444}.cell.permitted{background:#1a2a1a;color:#44ff44;border:1px solid #44ff44}.cell.identity{background:#1a1a3a;color:#4488ff;border:2px solid #4488ff}.big-number{font-size:4rem;font-weight:bold;margin:10px 0}.ratio{font-size:2rem;color:#ffd700;margin:30px 0;font-weight:bold}.explanation{max-width:700px;margin-top:30px;padding:20px;background:#1a1a2e;border-radius:12px;font-size:.95rem;line-height:1.6;color:#aaa;text-align:left}</style></head><body><h1>🔒 The Bottleneck Theorem</h1><h2>Self-loops at Perfect vs Imperfect Consonances</h2><div class='row'><div class='card perfect'><h3 style='color:#ffd700'>Perfect Fifth (P5)</h3><p style='color:#888;margin-bottom:10px'>Self-loops: voice leadings (β,σ) where β=σ</p><div class='grid' id='perfect-grid'></div><div class='big-number' style='color:#ffd700'>1</div><p>Only the identity (0,0) is permitted</p></div><div class='card imperfect'><h3 style='color:#00e5ff'>Minor Third (m3)</h3><p style='color:#888;margin-bottom:10px'>Self-loops: voice leadings (β,σ) where β=σ</p><div class='grid' id='imperfect-grid'></div><div class='big-number' style='color:#00e5ff'>12</div><p>All diagonal motions permitted</p></div></div><div class='ratio'>Ratio: 12 : 1</div><div class='explanation'><strong>Why?</strong> A self-loop at interval <em>j</em> requires σ = β (both voices move by the same amount). For <strong>perfect</strong> consonances, this is <em>parallel motion</em> — forbidden unless β = 0. Only the identity survives. For <strong>imperfect</strong> consonances, there is no parallel-motion restriction, so all 12 values of β work. This 12:1 ratio is the mathematical essence of the prohibition against parallel fifths and octaves.</div><script>const pg=document.getElementById('perfect-grid'),ig=document.getElementById('imperfect-grid');for(let b=0;b<12;b++){const d1=document.createElement('div');d1.className='cell '+(b===0?'identity permitted':'forbidden');d1.textContent=b===0?'✓ (0,0)':'✗ ('+b+','+b+')';d1.title=b===0?'Identity: permitted':'Parallel into P5: forbidden';pg.appendChild(d1);const d2=document.createElement('div');d2.className='cell permitted';d2.textContent='✓ ('+b+','+b+')';d2.title='No parallel restriction for imperfect consonances';ig.appendChild(d2)}</script></body></html>"
